  "answer_core": "I would not choose the hybrid direction yet. Treat the next two weeks as a short, low-cost test rather than a commitment. The real decision turns on two gates.\n\nFirst, check whether the Silva direction can actually become a dissertation: confirm you can get early data on a clear timeline, identify a collaborator who can compensate for the lab’s single-cell gaps, and get explicit support from your advisor rather than assuming it. Use Dr. Silva only as a low-cost reality check on concrete data access, collaboration depth, and timing. Ask what data exists, who controls it, when you can inspect it, what authorship or collaboration terms would look like, and what work would still be yours.\n\nSecond, keep the fallback alive while you test Silva. It only remains a fallback if your advisor keeps it advisor-backed, resourced, technically ready, identity-coherent, and available for you to re-enter, and if you set a clear stop date with visible conditions for saying no that serve as a stop-loss plan. In parallel, test whether the fallback can genuinely carry a dissertation if the Silva path fails.\n\nKeep the political sequence clean. Talk to your advisor first. Coordinate with your advisor before you involve the committee. Go to the committee only after you know whether the data path is real and whether your advisor would support the move without quietly pulling you back toward the safer lab path.\n\nFor the next two weeks, do dated evidence checks instead of building a finished proposal. Run the Silva probe strictly within the short window you define, and make the continuation decision only after you have actual evidence on both gates.\n\nDo not run all three options in parallel. Do not treat broad PhD success-rate claims as calibrated probabilities for this case; use them only as humility checks. The best path is not the hottest story; it is the one with a real chance of becoming a finishable PhD.",
